\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Induced action and interactions}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Integrating all fields produces a scalar that depends on the
supplied graph weights and mass. Its derivatives measure fluctuations
of edge differences; its determinant also counts rooted forests.
These are two descriptions of the same finite Gaussian normalization.
The [selected-background comparison](fdip-0059) distinguishes changes
of the supplied data from coordinate changes. In this full-field
integral the selector plays no role.}

\p{The four-cycle makes the derivative and forest formulas explicit.
The circle example then keeps the finite determinant and its chosen
normalization visible while taking a mesh limit. No continuum measure
or canonical infinite-dimensional determinant is needed for that limit.}

\p{Keeping a selected field and integrating its complement instead
produces an interaction on the retained space. Conditional means and
covariances determine its formal coefficients. Their transport follows
the same background arrow, provided it also transports the supplied
interaction. A quartic cycle example shows how graph weights and the
selector enter the induced vertices, without asserting convergence
of the perturbation series. For polynomial interactions bounded below,
[[fdip-00GG]] gives positive real-coupling laws, exact retained
weights and finite-order error estimates for these coefficients.}

